1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,420
Ten people liked it. One did not.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:07,300
Guess which one you took home. The nine you can barely remember.

3
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:11,220
The one you can quote word for word. You have a name for it already.

4
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:14,180
Too sensitive. Too thin skinned.

5
00:00:14,540 --> 00:00:18,060
But the count is not a character flaw. It is a measurement.

6
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:22,580
Your mind runs a permanent weighting system called negativity bias,

7
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:27,190
and inside it one hostile voice does not weigh the same as one kind voice,

8
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:29,600
and the exchange rate is not close.

9
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:33,320
By the end you will know what that rate actually is, who set it,

10
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:37,700
and the one question that decides whether a stranger gets your evening or not.

11
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:43,500
Start with the size of the effect, because it is bigger than it feels like it should be.

12
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:49,810
In 2001 a team of psychologists led by Roy Baumeister set out to find an exception.

13
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:53,360
They pulled together evidence from everywhere anyone had looked.

14
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,620
First impressions. Money won and money lost.

15
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:01,800
Feedback at work. Good days and bad days.

16
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:05,840
How new parents feel. How couples survive or do not.

17
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:11,400
They were looking for one domain where a good event of a certain size outweighed a bad event of

18
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:14,450
the same size. They did not find one.

19
00:01:14,580 --> 00:01:19,060
The paper is called Bad Is Stronger Than Good, and that title is not a mood.

20
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:25,120
It is the result. Across almost everything measured, a bad thing of a given size hits harder,

21
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:30,800
lasts longer, and shapes what you do next more than a good thing of exactly equal size.

22
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:33,870
And it happens far too fast to be a decision you are making.

23
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:39,900
In 1998 Tiffany Ito and her colleagues sat people down with electrodes on their scalps and

24
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:44,410
showed them pictures. Pleasant ones, neutral ones, unpleasant ones,

25
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:50,380
matched so the pleasant and unpleasant images were equally intense, just in opposite directions.

26
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:55,970
Then they watched the electrical response of the brain as each image landed.

27
00:01:56,170 --> 00:01:58,920
The negative images produced a bigger response.

28
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:01,660
Not a bigger opinion afterwards.

29
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:05,070
A bigger electrical event, inside the first second,

30
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:08,300
before the person had consciously judged anything at all.

31
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:13,749
Their paper put it plainly. Negative information weighs more heavily on the brain.

32
00:02:13,829 --> 00:02:16,740
So by the time you are deciding whether the comment was fair,

33
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:18,820
the weighting has already happened.

34
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:20,820
You are not choosing to give it more room.

35
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:23,680
You are being handed it with more room already attached.

36
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:26,670
Which leaves the obvious question. How much more?

37
00:02:26,810 --> 00:02:29,740
The clearest number comes from a place you would not expect,

38
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:31,800
which is people who love each other.

39
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:36,520
For decades John Gottman brought couples into a lab built to look like an apartment and recorded

40
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:39,520
them arguing. Not performing happiness.

41
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:43,960
Arguing, about money and in-laws and dishes, while cameras ran.

42
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:48,060
Then he tracked those couples for years to see who stayed together.

43
00:02:48,350 --> 00:02:51,160
The couples who lasted were not the couples who never fought.

44
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:53,220
They were the couples running a surplus.

45
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:58,760
During conflict itself, stable couples produced around five positive moments for every negative

46
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:02,940
one. A joke, a touch, a small yes,

47
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:06,310
an acknowledgement that the other person had a point.

48
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:11,340
The couples heading for separation were running closer to one to one, and often below it.

49
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:14,970
Five to one. In a bond where both people chose each other,

50
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:20,640
know each other, and are trying. That is the exchange rate your mind is working with.

51
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:25,720
Now hold that next to your afternoon. One stranger left one comment.

52
00:03:26,049 --> 00:03:30,280
To level it out, at that rate, you would need five specific, personal,

53
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:32,920
warm things to happen to you before the end of the day.

54
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:38,430
How many did you get? And the nine people who liked it did not each hand you one.

55
00:03:38,510 --> 00:03:41,440
Most of them handed you a silence you never noticed.

56
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:45,210
That is also why you can still quote the comment in the voice it was said in.

57
00:03:45,420 --> 00:03:48,300
The kind ones were received once and filed.

58
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:51,270
The other one was replayed on the way home, in the shower,

59
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:56,380
at eleven at night, and every replay laid it down again a little deeper.

60
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:58,860
You did not choose to give it that many hours.

61
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:01,860
It was already flagged as important before you got a vote.

62
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:07,260
Before you decide this makes you fragile, look at the world the weighting was set in,

63
00:04:07,340 --> 00:04:09,560
because nobody set it carelessly.

64
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:12,580
For almost all of human history you lived in a band.

65
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:17,580
Thirty people, forty, at the outer edge a network of about a hundred and fifty.

66
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:22,760
Not thirty people you had chosen. Thirty people you were born among and would die among,

67
00:04:22,840 --> 00:04:27,780
who fed you when your hunt failed and carried you when you were sick and were the entire supply

68
00:04:27,860 --> 00:04:32,220
of everything you needed. In that world, what is a hostile comment?

69
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:35,780
It is not an opinion. It is a reading of your standing.

70
00:04:35,860 --> 00:04:39,560
Somebody in the group is now speaking about you in a way that lowers you,

71
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:43,380
and lowering is not a feeling in that world, it is a share.

72
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:45,790
Of meat. Of protection.

73
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:50,300
Of who is willing to travel with you when the water dries up.

74
00:04:50,380 --> 00:04:52,880
And a comment does not sit still.

75
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:57,290
Christopher Boehm described the ladder small groups use to control their own members,

76
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:00,420
and it starts almost gently. Gossip.

77
00:05:00,740 --> 00:05:03,920
Then ridicule. Then people stop including you.

78
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,040
Then, at the top, exclusion from the band, which was not a bad month.

79
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:12,060
Alone, on foot, in that landscape, it was usually death.

80
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:14,769
So the first hostile voice was the first rung.

81
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,540
Reacting to it immediately, taking it home, turning it over all night, was not oversensitivity.

82
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:24,190
It was maintenance on the only life support system there was.

83
00:05:24,270 --> 00:05:28,680
There is an anthropologist who lived inside that machinery and wrote down what it does to a

84
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:34,920
person. In the 1960s Jean Briggs spent seventeen months with a small group of Utku Inuit in the

85
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:40,680
Canadian Arctic, adopted into a family as a daughter, living in the tent, learning the language.

86
00:05:41,380 --> 00:05:44,130
The Utku had an unusual relationship with anger.

87
00:05:44,210 --> 00:05:49,460
They did not express it. Not in raised voices, not in confrontation, not at children,

88
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:55,120
barely at all. Then some visiting outsiders borrowed a canoe and treated it badly,

89
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:58,970
and Briggs, on the group's behalf, spoke to them sharply.

90
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:03,700
The Utku did not shout at her. They did something quieter

91
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:07,920
and far heavier. They went cool.

92
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:13,279
She was still fed. She was still spoken to when speaking was necessary.

93
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:17,420
But the warmth was gone and the inclusion was gone,

94
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:22,930
and it stayed gone for about three months. Here is the part worth sitting with.

95
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:27,180
Briggs was a Harvard-trained researcher with an aeroplane out,

96
00:06:27,620 --> 00:06:32,760
a university behind her, a whole other world where hundreds of people thought well of her.

97
00:06:32,840 --> 00:06:34,899
She knew the ostracism was temporary.

98
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:39,380
She knew exactly what was happening and why, because studying it was her job.

99
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:43,950
It devastated her anyway. She wrote about it for the rest of her career.

100
00:06:44,030 --> 00:06:45,940
That is the strength of the machinery.

101
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:51,270
Even with an exit, even with an explanation, even with a whole other life waiting,

102
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:56,860
being frozen out by a handful of people you live among reorganises everything inside you.

103
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:01,840
Your ancestors had no exit, no explanation, and no other life.

104
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:07,740
Five to one starts to look conservative. And notice what the Utku were not doing.

105
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:13,140
Nobody insulted her. Nobody raised a voice or issued a warning or explained the rule she had

106
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:16,800
broken. The signal was carried entirely by warmth being withdrawn,

107
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:21,640
which is the cheapest thing a group can do and the most expensive thing a member can receive.

108
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:23,940
Your mind still scans for it constantly,

109
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:28,640
which is why a short reply from someone you love can occupy a whole evening while a long

110
00:07:28,720 --> 00:07:31,280
friendly one occupies none of it.

111
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:35,220
Now open your phone. Every part of that system is still running,

112
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:37,700
and the thing feeding it has changed completely.

113
00:07:38,060 --> 00:07:40,660
The voice in the comments is not in your band.

114
00:07:40,740 --> 00:07:43,480
They have not fed you. They will not carry you.

115
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:49,060
They will never see you again, and nothing that happens to you tomorrow costs them anything.

116
00:07:49,140 --> 00:07:53,380
In the world your alarm was built for, that combination did not exist.

117
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:56,250
A person who spoke badly of you was, without exception,

118
00:07:56,330 --> 00:07:58,990
a person who had to live beside the result.

119
00:07:59,070 --> 00:08:02,270
That was the whole reason their words were information.

120
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:04,060
And there is a second change, which is worse,

121
00:08:04,140 --> 00:08:06,860
because it decides which comment reaches you at all.

122
00:08:07,220 --> 00:08:13,140
In 2017 William Brady, Jay Van Bavel and their colleagues took more than half a million tweets

123
00:08:13,220 --> 00:08:18,180
about gun control, same-sex marriage, and climate change, and counted the words.

124
00:08:18,510 --> 00:08:22,500
They found that each additional moral or emotional word in a message raised its spread by

125
00:08:22,580 --> 00:08:25,460
roughly twenty percent. Not its truth.

126
00:08:25,550 --> 00:08:28,090
Not its representativeness. Its spread.

127
00:08:28,540 --> 00:08:30,920
Sit with what that means for your afternoon.

128
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:34,940
The comment that reached you was not a fair sample of what people think about you.

129
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:39,859
It was the one shaped to travel, and what travels is the sharpest wording available.

130
00:08:39,960 --> 00:08:44,660
The nine kind opinions did not have that property, so most of them never became words at all.

131
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:47,140
Your mind then took that filtered, selected,

132
00:08:47,220 --> 00:08:51,240
deliberately sharpened voice and processed it as though it were a member of your band speaking

133
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:53,900
at the fire. That is the mismatch.

134
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:58,480
Not that you weight bad news heavily. That was correct and it kept your line alive.

135
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:03,490
It is that the weighting was calibrated for people who bore the cost of being wrong about you,

136
00:09:03,570 --> 00:09:06,560
and it is now being applied to people who bear no cost at all.

137
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:10,120
So here is the shift, and it is one question, not a discipline.

138
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:14,300
When something someone said is still with you tonight, do not ask whether it was fair.

139
00:09:14,380 --> 00:09:16,800
Fairness is an argument, and you will lose it,

140
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:20,120
because you will argue both sides yourself until two in the morning.

141
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:22,780
Ask instead whether that person has to see you tomorrow.

142
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:25,680
That is the whole move. Not thicker skin.

143
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:30,140
Better routing. Because the question separates the two things that have been jammed together in

144
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:34,600
one channel. A friend, a partner, a colleague, your sister,

145
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:38,060
anyone who has to live with the result of being wrong about you,

146
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:43,100
is inside the band, and their hostile comment is real information and deserves the full weight

147
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:46,480
your mind is already giving it. Listen to that one properly.

148
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:50,700
It is expensive to them to say, which is exactly what makes it worth something.

149
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:53,940
And the stranger is not withholding information from you.

150
00:09:54,250 --> 00:09:58,080
They never had any. They did not see the nine people who liked it.

151
00:09:58,330 --> 00:10:00,530
They will not be there on Thursday.

152
00:10:00,610 --> 00:10:05,480
They paid nothing to say it, and a thing that costs nothing to say was never evidence about your

153
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:09,320
standing anywhere. You are not going to stop feeling the sting.

154
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:13,110
That is not on offer, and anyone promising it is selling something.

155
00:10:13,190 --> 00:10:17,840
The sting arrives before you do, on electrodes, in under a second.

156
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:20,670
But the sting is the alarm, not the verdict,

157
00:10:20,760 --> 00:10:24,180
and you get to decide who is allowed to be the verdict.

158
00:10:24,440 --> 00:10:26,920
Ten people liked it. One did not.

159
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,940
The one you took home was not the truest voice in the room.

160
00:10:30,020 --> 00:10:34,780
It was simply the loudest one to a system built for a world where every voice belonged to

161
00:10:34,860 --> 00:10:37,850
somebody who had to feed you in winter.

162
00:10:37,930 --> 00:10:40,940
The nine were your band and they were quiet,

163
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:47,340
because being liked has never made a sound. That silence is not absence.

164
00:10:48,100 --> 00:10:52,360
It is nine people who have to see you tomorrow, and who are fine with that.

165
00:10:53,580 --> 00:10:57,350
Now here is a question to take with you, and it is not a rhetorical one.

166
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:02,400
That sentence you can still quote word for word, in the voice it was said in.

167
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:05,950
How many times have you replayed it since it was actually said, six times,

168
00:11:06,260 --> 00:11:08,700
twenty, more than you would admit?

169
00:11:09,420 --> 00:11:11,660
Because not one of those was you listening to it again.

170
00:11:11,740 --> 00:11:15,460
Every single one was you writing it down again, a little sharper, a little colder,

171
00:11:15,540 --> 00:11:19,940
in a voice you supplied yourself. Nobody is making anything up here and something was certainly

172
00:11:20,020 --> 00:11:24,180
said. But the version burning you tonight has been through your hands far more than theirs,

173
00:11:24,260 --> 00:11:27,600
and there is a whole video about what that does to a memory.

174
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:30,160
It is on your screen right now.

175
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:32,460
Why you remember things that never happened.

176
00:11:32,540 --> 00:11:33,760
Watch that one next.
